@meta
id: 24-01
title: The shape of a command
chapter: 24
type: lesson
skills: ex-delete
par: 14
minutes: 4
vscode: scroll to it, click, shift-click, Delete
open: orders.fixture.ts

@brief
A bad merge left the `ord_1002` fixture in the file twice. The second
copy, lines 14 to 19, has to go. The first copy and everything around
it stays exactly as it is.

You have typed a lot of colon commands by now: `:wq`, `:s`, `:g`, `:bn`.
This chapter is about what they have in common. Every one of them has
the same three parts: a *range* that says which lines, a *command* that
says what to do, and an *argument* that says how. `:%s/a/b/g` is range
`%`, command `s`, argument `/a/b/g`. `:wq` is no range, command `wq`,
no argument.

The command here is `d`, delete. It is the same delete as `dd`, but it
takes a range instead of a motion, which means you do not need to move
the cursor to the lines first. Give it the two line numbers and press
Enter.

@keys
| `:14,19d` | delete lines 14 to 19, wherever the cursor is |
| `:d` | delete the current line |
| `:5d` | delete line 5 |

@hints
The line numbers are down the left edge. The block to remove starts at
the second `{` for `ord_1002` and ends at its `},`.
--
Two line numbers separated by a comma, then `d`. Nothing else. The
cursor can stay on line 1.
--
`:14,19d` and Enter, then `:wq`.

@solution
    :14,19d<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Twelve keystrokes, and the cursor never left line 1.

`14G6dd` would have done it in six. That is not the point. The point is
that `:14,19d` is a sentence in a small language, and every command in
this chapter is the same sentence with a different verb. `:14,19m0`
moves those lines to the top. `:14,19t$` copies them to the bottom.
`:14,19>` indents them. `:14,19j` joins them. Once the shape is in your
hands, each new command costs you one letter to learn.

The range can be a single line (`:5d`), two lines (`:14,19d`), or
nothing at all, which means the current line (`:d`). `.` is the current
line, `$` the last, `%` the whole file, and a mark works too (`:'a,'bd`).
Chapter 25 adds patterns and offsets to that list. For now, line numbers
are enough, and they are right there in the gutter.

